  "slug": "auxin-topic-eb88",
  "summary": "Five preprints define domain-specific auxin perception, SBP-mediated repression and polarized expansin delivery as spatial determinants of plant organ form.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is spatial restriction of developmental signals in roots, with one floral counterpart. Domain-specific synthetic auxin perception in Arabidopsis roots indicates that outer elongating cells control root growth, and shifting the domain moves the proliferation zone. In Hibiscus trionum petals, auxin preserves bullseye proportions through distal cell behaviour but does not initiate the boundaries. In maize, SBP factors TSH4, UB2 and UB3 repress brace root initiation and primordia expansion via LG2 and gibberellin signalling. ROP activation recruits EXPA7 to root hair initiation sites by brefeldin-A-sensitive trafficking. Metaxylem number varies along wheat and grass root axes, linked to hydraulic modelling. Together these works separate initiation from proportion and expansion.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
